Sure, here's an example implementation of `Code74k-express.ts` using KickLang to create a simple Express server:

```javascript
import express from 'express';
import { fileURLToPath } from 'url';
import { dirname, join } from 'path';
import { Code74k } from './Code74k.js';

const app = express();
const port = 3000;

// Serve static files from the 'public' directory
app.use(express.static('public'));

// Parse JSON bodies
app.use(express.json());

// Load Code74k data from a file
const dataPath = join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), 'Code74k.json');
const code74kData = require(dataPath);

// Define a route to get all Code74k entries
app.get('/Code74k', (req, res) => {
  res.json(code74kData);
});

// Define a route to get a specific Code74k entry by key
app.get('/Code74k/:key', (req, res) => {
  const key = req.params.key;
  const entry = code74kData.find((entry) => entry.key === key);

  if (entry) {
    res.json(entry);
  } else {
    res.status(404).json({ error: 'Entry not found' });
  }
});

// Start the server
app.listen(port, () => {
  console.log(`Server started on http://localhost:${port}`);
});
```

This code sets up a basic Express server that serves Code74k data. It includes two routes: one to retrieve all Code74k entries and another to retrieve a specific entry by its key. The Code74k data is loaded from a JSON file (`Code74k.json`) located in the same directory as the `Code74k-express.ts` file.

Make sure to have the necessary dependencies installed by running `npm install express path` before running this code.

To use this code, save it as `Code74k-express.ts` and run it using Node.js. The server will start on localhost at port 3000, and you can access the defined routes to retrieve Code74k data.
